# Your First Charts

A chart is really just shapes placed in the right spots. In this chapter we take the small dataset on the left and turn it into our first charts — starting with the canvas everything is drawn on, then plotting each row as a point, and building up from there.

<Section id="svg" title="The SVG canvas">

Everything we draw lives on an **SVG** — a canvas you write with _tags_, the same building blocks as a web page. A tag is a keyword in angle brackets like `<svg>`, and most come in pairs: an opening `<svg>` and a closing `</svg>`, with whatever we draw nested between them.

Each tag carries **attributes** — `name="value"` settings. For the canvas we set a width and a height, in pixels:

```svelte
/// file: An SVG canvas
<svg class="chart" width="600" height="400"></svg>
```

<CanvasDemo />

We can see that empty box only because `class="chart"` gives it a light background and border — an SVG with nothing drawn in it is invisible by default.

### The coordinate system

Every position is measured from the **top-left corner**, `(0, 0)`. `x` grows to the **right**, and `y` grows **downward** — so a bigger `y` sits _lower_ on the canvas, the opposite of a maths graph. Keep that in mind the moment we start placing shapes.

{#snippet sidebar()}

> [!WARNING]
> An empty SVG is invisible. We add a light background + border (the `chart` class) so you can see the canvas while you work.

> [!WARNING]
> Coordinates start at the **top-left** `(0, 0)`, and `y` increases **downward**.

Copy this to start your canvas:

```svelte
/// file: SVG canvas
<svg class="chart" width="600" height="400"></svg>
```

{/snippet}

</Section>

<Section id="circles" title="Plotting circles">

Now we draw inside the canvas. A point is a `<circle>`, which needs a centre — `cx`, `cy` — and a radius `r`:

```svelte
/// file: A circle
<circle cx="100" cy="80" r="8" fill="steelblue" />
```

That places a single dot. But we have a whole table of rows, and we'd rather not write a `<circle>` by hand for each. Svelte's `{#each}` block loops over the data and repeats the markup once per row, and curly braces `{ }` let us drop in a little maths to turn a value into a pixel position:

```svelte
/// file: One circle per row
{#each data as d}
	<circle cx={d.x * 6} cy={d.y * 4} r="8" fill="steelblue" />
{/each}
```

Our `x` and `y` columns run from 0 to 100, but the canvas is 600×400 — so we scale `x` up by 6 and `y` by 4 to spread the points across it. We work in **pixels** like this rather than percentages, so the maths stays explicit.

Drop that loop inside your `<svg>` and you've drawn a scatter plot — the goal is on the left.

{#snippet sidebar()}

A single circle:

```svelte
/// file: Circle
<circle cx="100" cy="80" r="8" fill="steelblue" />
```

One circle per data row:

```svelte
/// file: Loop
{#each data as d}
	<circle cx={d.x * 6} cy={d.y * 4} r="8" fill="steelblue" />
{/each}
```

**Goal**

<ScatterDemo />

{/snippet}

